--- docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v68a-draft.txt	2026-10-31 13:51:32
+++ docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v70-draft.txt	2026-10-31 14:50:20
@@ -5,6 +5,18 @@
 - New (1): a new event or step, a new figure, a new forecast or plan, a new policy step, a new specific judgement ("the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity"; "the regulator approved the drug for a second indication"; "the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027"). Guidance, and a forecast or a plan still in force, count when retold; a retold past actual figure is optional; a development the item does not show to be old is not a retelling. Not new: an arrangement the item only describes as in place, with no new step, even when no date is given ("two airlines fly the planemaker's jets"; "the bank works with a payments firm on cards"); the company's history ("founded in 1998"); a claim everyone following the company makes without a figure and a specific account (that it leads, that demand is strong, that supply is tight: "rivals have spent billions but still lag"); a figure the item itself presents as already known, unless it is guidance or a forecast or plan still in force.
 - Moves (2): name the question and the path, for example demand, supply and input costs, competition and share, pricing and installed base, product progress, policy, or capital and obligations ("a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe" moves the company's pricing; "the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth" moves its demand). In links, name an open question's id when one fits, or your own candidate's id when the statement raises a new question; leave links empty when the question it moves is neither open nor worth opening (a customer's, a supplier's or a macro question), and write it in moves. Another company's action or figure needs such a path to a question about the company; a party next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem moves nothing. A statement that moves no question is not evidence, however specific.
 - Not kept (3), each row with its boundary: a product, game, driver or benchmark detail with no business scale, and a product comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes ("the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge"; "the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones"); not a competitor's comparison against the company's product, nor a partner's or customer's new adoption step in the same sentence, nor the launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of the product that carries the company's value, nor the company's decision to change the price it charges: those go back to (1) and (2). A share-price move or an investor's position ("shares fell 13% this month"); the reaction after results is optional; the news behind a move goes back to (1) and (2). An unargued view, an author's general risk list or a quote that only characterises ("the stock is a hold"; "if demand slows it would hurt"; "a pivotal moment"), and a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no fact or calculation; a reading that adds one goes back to (1) and (2); a conditional valuation with its assumptions, derivation and date is kept. A political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision; an official's order, law or ruling is a policy step and goes back to (1) and (2). Another company's valuation, share price or deal that touches none of the company's markets; its actions and operating figures go back to (2). The same fact told again in other words (one deal, hire, programme or reported measure with its terms, figures and growth rates is one evidence item). Boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text, advertising and page material unrelated to the item.
+- What noise looks like, and what the evidence next to it looks like. Each left side is not kept; each right side passes the three questions:
+  - an arrangement retold: "two airlines fly the planemaker's jets" | a new step: "a third airline ordered 40 of the jets for 2028 delivery";
+  - management cheer: "the CEO called the quarter spectacular and the pipeline the strongest ever" | guidance: "the CEO said full-year revenue will be $12–13 billion";
+  - an author's or analyst's view: "the stock looks cheap"; "this is a buying opportunity" | a sourced estimate: "an analyst valued the shares at $80, 15 times next year's earnings of $5.30";
+  - a trend remark: "demand for cloud capacity is accelerating"; "financing is becoming the constraint" | a figure or a dated step: "the largest customer raised its 2026 capital spending plan to $190 billion";
+  - a product boast: "the new engine burns 20% less fuel"; "the update loads reports 30% faster" | business scale: "the airline took delivery of 12 and ordered 30 more";
+  - a political or strategic stance: "the minister said the government's position is consistent" | a policy step: "the ministry issued the import licence on 8 July";
+  - a general risk: "if demand slows the company would suffer" | a stated change: "the main supplier cut its 2027 output plan by 15%";
+  - another company's own matter with no path to the company: "a sensor maker cut its unit cost from $10,000 to $200" | the same company with a path: "the sensor maker's unit was chosen for the company's vehicle platform";
+  - a reading of an event already recorded: "the delay opens a window for rivals" | the event itself: "the delay pushes first shipments from 2027 to 2028";
+  - a reporter's framing: "the company, founded in 1998, dominates its market" | nothing: framing yields no item.
+  A statement shaped like a left side is kept only when the item adds what the right side has.
 - One matter, one item: each matter the analyst would note is its own evidence item, with its own terms and figures, even when several sit in one paragraph (a revenue figure and a capex plan in one sentence are two items; one deal with its amount, term and parties is one); words of another matter inside a verbatim quote are no harm, but no matter hides inside another's item. A page with eight statements that pass yields eight evidence items, not a selection; a dense page yields many; a page with none yields none.
 
 How to write evidence

